"_____________________________________________________________"
"Bern uses a Odin-like syntax for loops."
"This means that the only word for loops is \"loop\""
"And the way you type it changes how the loop behaves."
"(\"for\" is still accepted as an alias for compatibility.)"
"_____________________________________________________________\n"

"[1. Repeat Loop]"
"The \"Repeat Loop\" repeats an action a specified number of times:"
"loop 3 do"
"    \"Hello, Loops!\""
"end"

"\nResult:"
loop 3 do
    "Hello, Loops!"
end

"\n_____________________________________________________________\n"


"[2. Conditional Loop]"
"The \"Conditional Loop\" repeats an action until the conditional is false:"
"counter = 2"
"loop counter > 0 do"
"    \"Hello, Loops!\""
"    counter = counter - 1"
"end"

"\nResult:"
counter = 2
loop (counter > 0) do
    "Hello, Loops!"
    counter = counter - 1
end

"\n_____________________________________________________________\n"
"[3. Infinite Loop]"
"To do a Infinite Loop, use loop true like this:"
"loop true do"
"   \"Hello!\""
"end"

-- Commented to run normally.
-- loop true do
--    "Hello!"
-- end


"\n_____________________________________________________________\n"
"[3. Loop-In]"
"A Loop-In can be used to iterate every object of a List, Set or String."
"Consider the example:"
"text = \"Bern\""
"loop char : text do"
"   \"Current char is:\" + char"
"end"

"\nResult:"

text = "Bern"
loop char : text do
   "Current char is: " + char
end

"\n_____________________________________________________________\n"
"[3. Loop-In with Counter]"
"An automatic enumeration is also available with the following syntax:"
"Consider the example:"
"text = \"Bern\""
"loop char, index : text do"
"   \"Current char is:\" + char"
"   \"Current index is:\" + index"
"end"

"\nResult:"

textt = "Bern"
loop char, index : textt do
   "Current char is: " + char
    "Current index is: " + index
end
